    <h2>The Three Housing Bands That Run the Work</h2>

    <p>Most contractors in Huntsville quote work the same way whether the call is coming from a 1920s bungalow in Five Points or a 2019 build in Edgewater. That's a mistake, and a website that doesn't acknowledge the three distinct housing bands of this city looks like it was written for a contractor in any other Alabama town.</p>

    <p>The historic core — Twickenham, Old Town, Five Points, the bungalow stock around Lincoln Mill — is pre-1940 construction. Cast iron drain stacks, original heart pine floors, lath-and-plaster walls, knob-and-tube remnants in the deepest corners of the oldest houses. The homeowners here actively pay premium for period-correct restoration work. They will reject a contractor who suggests vinyl siding or LVP on their front rooms. They want the boards, the trim profiles, the paint protocols. Twickenham and Old Town also fall under city design review, which means certain exterior decisions require approval before work begins.</p>

    <p>The middle band — Holmes Hill, McThornmor Acres, Drake Avenue corridor, Bailey Cove, Brahan Spring, Blossomwood, Whitesburg Estates, the long sweep of South Huntsville — is the 1950s-1980s ranch and split-level stock. This is where most of the high-frequency trade work in the city actually happens. Original HVAC systems hitting 18-25 years. Original water heaters past their 12-year mark. Original 200-amp panels with split-bus arrangements pre-dating GFCI requirements. The homeowners in this band are 30 years older than the original buyers, which means the work coming up is replacement at scale.</p>

    <p>The outer band — Hays Farm, Edgewater, Village of Providence, Mid-City, the new construction along Chapman Mountain — is mostly post-2010 builder construction. Systems are still mostly under warranty. The work here is irrigation install, fence build, fresh landscape, garage organization, smart-home retrofits. The recurring revenue is in the HOA-driven contracts: monthly mow-and-blow, quarterly pest, annual pressure washing. Lower per-job ticket, higher consistency.</p>

    <h2>Where the Customers Actually Live</h2>

    <p>Pull up the map for a second. Huntsville's metro population pushed past 500,000 in the early 2020s and crossed 225,000 inside the city limits in the 2020 census. The growth came from aerospace, defense contracting, software, and the Mazda Toyota plant employment spill from Limestone County. Median household income runs well above the state average. The population skews younger than every other Alabama market because of the engineering employer base.</p>

    <p>That density is unevenly distributed. The high-earning households cluster in a band that runs from Green Mountain on the southeast — large homes on bluff lots with Tennessee Valley views — through Whitesburg Estates with its custom builds from $349K to $799K, through Blossomwood at the foot of Monte Sano, and west into Village of Providence and Hays Farm. These neighborhoods are where the premium upsell lives: variable-speed heat pumps instead of 13 SEER straight-cool, standing-seam metal instead of architectural shingle, custom hardscape instead of basic concrete.</p>

    <p>The volume work lives elsewhere. Chase Industrial corridor and the older stock along Saint Clair Avenue have heavy rental density, which means landlord-paid work that pays less per job but never stops. UAH's 10,000+ student population and Alabama A&M just to the north drive apartment density along Memorial Parkway. The rail-corridor and Beltline-era neighborhoods bring rodent and pest control demand at a different scale than the south-side enclaves. The Drake Avenue corridor — established 1960s-1980s mixed residential and commercial — is the meat-and-potatoes service area where most local trades earn their consistent calls.</p>

    <h2>Why Huntsville Search Is Harder Than Anywhere Else in Alabama</h2>

    <p>This part isn't fun to hear, but it matters. Search results for "plumber Huntsville AL" or "HVAC Huntsville" look nothing like the equivalent searches for Decatur or Athens. The top of the page is dominated by Google Ads bought by national lead-generation services. Below the ads come <a href="/articles/angi-leads/">Angi listings</a>, then Yelp, then Thumbtack, then a handful of local contractors with serious marketing budgets who started building search visibility before 2020. The local guy doing excellent work out of his shop near Drake Avenue is on page four. Invisible.</p>

    <p>That doesn't mean the game is lost. It means the strategy has to be different. The contractors who dominate Huntsville search built their positions by publishing real content for years — 30+ pages of service-area depth, 200+ Google reviews, consistent monthly additions to their site. The good news: that approach still works. Nothing about Google's algorithm has changed the fundamentals. The contractors who start the process in 2026 will be the ones dominating Huntsville search in 2028. The ones who wait will spend the next two years writing checks to Angi for the same handful of leads three competitors are also bidding on.</p>

